(Regression test for bug #68: it is not only nested lists. Every element of
 a mixed list came back through a format hole as the compiler's static
 guess - integer - whatever its runtime tag said: a rodata address for a
 text element, the raw IEEE-754 bits for a float. A UNIFORM list was fine
 only because the static guess happened to be right, which is why the bug
 hid.

 LANGUAGE.md ("Lists"): "Each element renders by its own type, not the
 list's."

 Acceptance: every row of the mixed lists renders as its own type, and the
 uniform lists - the rows that already worked - still do.)

a list called nested is [1, [2, 3], "four"].
a list called 'the mixed row' is [1, "two", 2.5].
a list called letters is ["a", "b"].
a list called scores is [10, 20].

(A mixed list, element by element.)
Print "{element 1 of nested}".
Print "{element 2 of nested}".
Print "{element 3 of nested}".
Print "{element 3 of 'the mixed row'}".

(The uniform controls, which the static guess already got right.)
Print "{element 1 of letters}".
Print "{element 1 of scores}".
